<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Lament · 1870 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A spiritual from the time of slavery in the United States, in which the singer feels as lost as a child without a mother.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>enslaved African Americans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Southern United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>era of slavery in the United States; sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers in the 1870s</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional African-American spiritual from the era of slavery)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sometimes-i-feel-like-a-motherless-child">Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child</a>&quot;, also sung as &quot;Motherless Child&quot;, is a traditional spiritual that goes back to the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. One early performance was by the Fisk Jubilee Singers in the 1870s. Samuel Coleridge-Taylor made it the first movement of his Trio in E minor in 1893, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> recorded it several times from 1926 onwards. In 1942 the Wings Over Jordan Choir recorded it for Columbia Masterworks, presenting it, and the spirituals as a whole, as American classical music.</p><p>It was often heard during the civil rights movement, and it has been recorded in many forms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahalia-jackson">Mahalia Jackson</a> recorded it in 1956, and Odetta sang it at Carnegie Hall on 8 April 1960; her version was heard in Pier Paolo Pasolini's film The Gospel According to St. Matthew (1964). On 15 August 1969 Richie Havens opened the Woodstock festival with a version he called &quot;Freedom (Motherless Child)&quot;. Thea Bowman, a Black Catholic religious sister, recorded it in 1988 and sang it the next year at a meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Conference of Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>, while giving a speech on Black Catholic history and experience, shortly before her death from cancer. The soprano Barbara Hendricks sang it when she received the Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> Award for the Arts in 2002, and John Legend sang it at the Hope for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> Now telethon in 2010.</p><p>Composers have built on it too: George Walker quoted it in his Piano Sonata No. 4, and Olly Wilson based Sometimes (1976), for tenor and tape, on it. Versions range from Boney M.'s disco &quot;Motherless Child&quot; (1977) to Van Morrison's, in which, according to his biographer Brian Hinton, the mother he misses is Ireland, and Ghostface Killah's hip-hop &quot;Motherless Child&quot; (1996).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of pain and despair, in which the singer compares his hopelessness to that of a child torn from its parents. In one reading the word &quot;sometimes&quot; holds a measure of hope: at other times, at least, the singer does not feel so lost.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius" data-genius="2881962"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press the button. <a href="https://genius.com/Paul-robeson-sometimes-i-feel-like-a-motherless-child-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
